) (Spark Docs) Governance whitelists key risk parameters (LLTVs, IRMs) and can toggle fee switch (capped) (docs.morpho.org)": "Parameter control is contract-architecture driven; validate upgradeability/admin keys per deployment (practitioner diligence item) (GitHub)", "Practitioner Validation Check YES creates credit onchain • YES liquidation rules enforced by contracts (HF<1) (aave.com) • YES positions unwindable programmatically YES creates credit onchain • YES liquidation enforced by contracts • YES positions unwindable programmatically YES creates credit onchain • YES liquidation enforced via protocol auctions • YES positions unwindable programmatically YES creates credit onchain • YES liquidation rules enforced (HF<1) (Spark Docs) • YES positions unwindable programmatically YES creates credit onchain • YES liquidation enforced (LTV>LLTV) (docs.morpho.org) • YES positions unwindable programmatically": "Credit creation: YES • Liquidation enforcement: YES (contract lifecycle) • Programmatic unwind: YES (term default path), but economic recovery depends on NFT liquidity (GitHub)", "Practitioner’s Note Research focus: liquidation mechanics (health factor dynamics), collateral mix concentration, oracle coverage/fallbacks, and governance latency during fast markets. Also track version/market fragmentation (v1 frozen vs v3 active) when analyzing risk. (messari.io) Research focus: differences between v2 and v3 risk profiles; base-asset concentration in v3; liquidation throughput under stress; governance response speed during incidents. Research focus: auction efficiency during stress, oracle latency, RWA exposure and offchain enforcement risk, governance concentration, and emergency shutdown assumptions. Watchpoints: Aave v3 fork deltas (custom DAI/sDAI logic), oracle stack and feed coverage, asset-list governance, and liquidation throughput under stablecoin stress. Also note Spark’s bug bounty posture. (Spark Docs) Watchpoints: oracle selection/config is a first-order risk surface (market-by-market). Validate LLTV choice, oracle robustness, and liquidation liquidity for collateral. Review post-mortems for oracle manipulation and operational incidents. (Morpho Governance Forum)": "Key diligence: enforceability of maturity/default transitions, collateral custody invariants, upgradeability/admin privileges, and recovery/liquidation path for collateral value realization. (GitHub)", "Primary Role in DeFi Base credit + leverage layer for Ethereum DeFi Base credit layer and leverage primitive for Ethereum DeFi Stablecoin issuance + base credit layer for DeFi DAI-centered credit & liquidity venue; major stablecoin + ETH collateral borrowing/supply rails inside Sky ecosystem (Blockworks) Base lending primitive for permissionless risk markets; “lending infrastructure” others build vaults/allocators on top of": "NFT-backed credit rail for high-value assets; term financing", "Protocol Status Active (v3 widely used; v1 largely frozen) (messari.io) Active (v3 “Comet” is primary; v2 largely deprecated/frozen for growth) Active (DAI issuance core to Ethereum DeFi) Active Active": "Active", "Reason for Inclusion Meets criteria: onchain borrowing/lending; collateralized credit issuance; onchain interest rate mechanics; liquidation + solvency enforced by protocol logic. (aave.com) Meets criteria: issues credit onchain against collateral; defines interest rates, liquidation logic, and solvency rules fully in smart contracts. Meets criteria: onchain borrowing; collateralized credit issuance; explicit liquidation and solvency logic; interest (stability fees) set and enforced onchain. Meets criteria: onchain borrowing/lending, collateralized credit issuance, onchain interest + liquidation + solvency rules. (Spark Docs) Meets criteria: creates credit and enforces liquidation/solvency fully onchain; interest and risk logic are protocol-defined per market. (docs.morpho.org)": "Fits criteria: collateral escrow onchain, credit issuance via loan contracts, and enforceable lifecycle/default handling in smart contracts. (GitHub)", "Systemic Risk Exposure High (systemically important liquidity + collateral hub) High (core, long-lived DeFi credit primitive with broad integrations) Very High (DAI is systemically embedded across DeFi and beyond) High (meaningful stablecoin and ETH market integration within Sky ecosystem) (Blockworks) High (rapidly becoming core lending primitive; systemic exposure rises with integration density)": "Low–Medium (more isolated; smaller systemic coupling)", "Upstream Dependencies DEX liquidity for liquidation execution; oracle networks; stablecoin liquidity; governance/risk stewards Oracles (Chainlink); DEX liquidity for liquidation execution; stablecoin liquidity Oracles; keeper/liquidation infrastructure; governance processes; real-world legal structures (for RWAs) Oracles; DEX liquidity for liquidation execution; Sky/Maker liquidity inputs; governance operations (Blockworks) Oracles; liquidator/keeper ecosystem; market curation/allocators; DEX liquidity for liquidation execution": "NFT custody/escrow contracts; marketplace liquidity affects recovery values"}, "source_label": "Lending Markets"}'::jsonb),
  ('liquity-v1', 'Liquity (V1)', 'defi-systems-architecture', 'lending-markets', 'liquity.org (Liquity)', ARRAY['Over-collateralized borrowing: lock ETH in a Trove', 'mint LUSD debt (Liquity Docs)']::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "ETH-collateralized borrowing protocol where users mint LUSD against ETH with liquidations primarily handled by the Stability Pool rather than auctions. (Liquity)", "extras": {"Collateral Types Supported ERC-20s; ETH; widely supports LST collateral in certain markets (risk-parameter dependent) ERC-20s; ETH (wrapped); selected stablecoins; LST support market-dependent (primarily via governance-approved assets) ETH; ERC-20s; selected LSTs; RWAs (via specialized vaults) ERC-20s + ETH (incl. stETH, DAI, sDAI at launch; asset set is governance/risk-managed) (TradingView) ERC-20s; market-defined collateral/loan pairs (including LSTs, stablecoins, and other ERC-20 collateral depending on market config) (docs.morpho.org)": "ETH collateral (V1); V2 docs show additional collateral markets, but V1 is ETH-only (Liquity)", "Dependency Concentration Risk High oracle reliance + large-collateral concentration risk (market-dependent); governance/parameter-change latency risk High oracle reliance; Medium–High asset concentration risk in v3 base asset; governance latency risk High oracle reliance; High governance concentration risk; Medium–High RWA dependency risk High oracle reliance + governance/parameter-change latency risk; asset concentration risk (DAI-centric design) (Spark Docs) High oracle dependence (per-market); configuration/parameter risk; integration/curation risk (markets can be permissionlessly created but quality varies) (mixbytes.io)": "High ETH-only collateral concentration; liquidation liquidity dependence (Stability Pool + ETH market depth); oracle/price-feed dependence (Liquity Docs)", "Downstream Consumers Yield strategies, leveraged LPs, hedged carry, structured products, derivatives margin users Yield strategies, leveraged positions, DAO treasuries, arbitrage and carry strategies DEX liquidity, lending protocols, yield strategies, derivatives, DAOs, payment rails Yield strategies, stablecoin allocators, leverage users, structured products integrating DAI liquidity Vaults/allocators, structured lending products, leveraged strategies, institutional deployers using Morpho rails": "Leverage loops, stablecoin liquidity strategies, DAO treasury ops, yield/structured products using LUSD (Liquity Docs)", "Governance Model Token governance (AAVE) via Aave DAO processes; proposals/voting for parameter changes (aave.com) Token governance (COMP) via Compound DAO proposals and voting Token governance (MKR) with executive votes and onchain spell execution Governance via SPK token; Snapshot/signaling and governance system documented by Spark (Spark Docs) Token governance via MORPHO; weighted voting and defined governance powers (docs.morpho.org)": "No parameter governance; LQTY exists for incentives/staking, but core V1 contracts are described as immutable (Aave)", "Historical Stress Events Yes (v1 largely frozen/disabled for new supply/borrow; legacy positions remain manageable) (messari.io) Yes (v2 experienced stress events and governance interventions; migration to v3 reflects risk simplification lessons) Yes (notably March 2020 “Black Thursday” auction failures; subsequent mechanism redesigns) No major protocol-loss event cited in core docs; treat as “monitor” (bug bounty active) (Immunefi) Yes (documented oracle-manipulation event on a specific market; plus a frontend-related incident where contracts unaffected) (Morpho Governance Forum)": "Monitor (mechanism stress typically expresses as Stability Pool depletion / redemption pressure rather than “hack-style” events) (Liquity Docs)", "Interest Rate Model Utilization-based variable rates; also supports “stable” borrow rate mode (market-specific) Utilization-based interest rate curves per asset (v2); simplified rate model with base/borrow asset separation (v3) Governance-set stability fees per vault type (effectively interest rates) Utilization-based IR model (Aave v3 lineage) + custom DAI/sDAI strategies in Spark context (Medium) Market-selected Interest Rate Models (IRMs); IRMs/LLTVs are constrained by governance whitelists (docs.morpho.org)": "“Interest-free” loans (no variable borrow rate); costs are via borrowing/redemption fee mechanics rather than utilization rates (Liquity Docs)", "Isolation of Risk Risk primarily at market/reserve parameter level (not isolated per-user); isolation features exist in newer versions/markets but not “isolated pools” by default Moderate isolation: v3 introduces asset isolation via a single base asset with segmented collateral; v2 uses shared pools High isolation at vault-type level; each collateral type has independent parameters Shared pool risk by asset/reserve parameters (not per-market isolation like Morpho Blue) High isolation: each market is isolated and immutable by design (docs.morpho.org)": "Risk is primarily at system level (shared Stability Pool + redistribution mechanisms), not isolated pools (Liquity Docs)", "Launch Year 2020 (v1 Ethereum mainnet) (aave.com) 2018 (v2 on Ethereum) 2017 (Single-Collateral DAI; Multi-Collateral DAI in 2019) 2023 (public launch May 9, 2023) (X (formerly Twitter)) 2023 (Morpho Blue introduced Oct 2023) (morpho.org)": "2021 (Ethereum mainnet launch: Apr 5, 2021) (Liquity)", "Lending Model Pooled liquidity markets (multi-asset) Pooled liquidity markets (v2); single-collateral base with isolated borrow assets (v3/Comet) CDP / Vault-based collateralized lending Pooled liquidity markets (Aave v3-derived money market) (Spark Docs) Permissionless, isolated lending markets (1 collateral + 1 loan asset per market) (docs.morpho.org)": "CDP / Trove-based borrowing (single-purpose: borrow against collateral) (Liquity)", "Liquidation Mechanism Onchain liquidations triggered when Health Factor < 1; liquidators repay debt for collateral + bonus (bonus/thresholds per asset, governance/risk-managed) (aave.com) Onchain liquidations when collateral value breaches borrow limits; liquidators repay debt for collateral at protocol-defined incentives Onchain liquidation via auctions (debt, collateral, surplus); keeper participation required Health Factor-based liquidations (HF < 1), onchain seizure/repay mechanics (Spark Docs) Standard liquidation when LTV exceeds LLTV; liquidators repay debt and seize collateral at discount (protocol-enforced) (docs.morpho.org)": "Stability Pool absorbs liquidations by burning LUSD to repay liquidated debt and receiving collateral (ETH) — primary liquidation path (Liquity Docs)", "Maintaining Organization Aave DAO (governance) + Aave Labs (major dev & interface/operator tooling) (aave.com) Compound DAO (governance) with historical development by Compound Labs MakerDAO (DAO-governed protocol) with core units / contributors maintaining modules Spark Governance (SPK) operating via the Spark Agent in the Sky Atlas (Sky ecosystem) (Spark Docs) Morpho Governance (MORPHO token); governance whitelists IRMs/LLTVs and can activate fee switch (docs.morpho.org)": "Immutable protocol (no governance parameter control); Liquity team maintains docs, integrations, and ecosystem communications (Liquity)", "Oracle Dependencies Primarily Chainlink price feeds with protocol risk controls (market-config dependent) Chainlink price feeds (primary), with protocol-defined fallbacks/guards Decentralized Oracle Network (Maker Oracles) with Chainlink integrations and redundancy Oracle-driven risk system; Spark has integrated oracle providers (e.g., RedStone) for lending markets (RedStone blog) Oracle per market; Morpho established an oracle wrapper for Chainlink feeds to support initial markets (morpho.org)": "Uses price inputs to enforce collateral ratios and liquidation thresholds (implementation details are protocol-specific; practitioners treat oracle/price feed assumptions as first-order risk) (Liquity